<BR>
<center><h1>Rules: Raids & Loot</h1></center>
<BR>
<table cellspacing=5 cellpadding=5>
	<tr><td><h2>1.</td><td><h2>Raid Credits - How to earn</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>Each mob is given a value of what it is worth in raid points. The guild's  Mob Watch system will be used to list the value of each mob. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>Using points for mobs allows us to split the 'raid night' up into smaller chunks. However it could be that one mob may take us all night - possibly due to wipes or tactic attempts and some members may not be there for the final kill. To overcome this we will implement the ability to score partially for a raid. This will be calculated based on taking the Mob value per hour MVPH (which is also stored on the mob watch database) and multiplying that by the number of hours a member was present for. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>The raid window is used to 'DUMP' to a file roughly each hour on the mob. The website log parser will be adapted to read the new dump layout and again feed the appropriate credits to the member for the mob. </td></tr>

	<tr><td colspan=2>Some members like to raid midweek, some weekend, some like to raid every now and again and some like to raid all the time. Unfortunately it is always a tricky situation. The easy option is to say you are totally casual to raiding or you can say you are a 24/7 raid guild like (Darkwind, RF) etc. EoD at it's peak was a weekend raid guild. Basically mid week we would grind, level, quest etc and weekkend Fri Sat Sun we would hit the big targets with rules that made a member attend IF online. This we will change slightly with the introduction of Mob Values. We don't necessarily need to only score raid nights we can score for mid week raids on particular mobs. Therefore we will remove the offial weekend raid nights and replace with an as and when approach. We will however retain our MUST attend rule for weekend raiding (if online). For mid week targets its entirely optional to attend. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If the guild leader is not present, a mob is up and the guild in general wish to kill it then they may organise the raid - inviting the guild (EoD) and ally resources to attend (noted allies only). The raid requester however must be approved by an officer online or be an officer himself. If it is midweek then members do not need to attend (optional). Care should be taken when addressing allys to EoD (such as Legado) as they are under NO obligation to attend. If the raid is on a weekend then EoD members should attend as long as stated the raid is approved by officers online and it is a target listed & valued in the MobWatch system. To run a raid you must take an EoD reasonable raid force with the intention to succeed. For example, you may not take 3 people to kill Yelinak, take 6 hours doing it and be awarded 2 points per hour for the attempt. Reasonable EoD force would be a number we have achieved the mob with to date or perhaps or 90 % of it if smaller.
					  If you are running the raid the log of the entire raid must be kept, zipped and emailed together with the raid window dumps per hour for the duration of the raid. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>It should be noted that only EoD, trial members and declared allies will be invited to raid with EoD. Trial members will have no rights to loot. Allies will use the points system as EoD do. Raid leaders may not invite guests to raid with EoD. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><b>Raid Credits Summary </b><BR>
						-Mob Total Value field on MobWatch <BR>
						-Mob Value per hour field on MobWatch <BR>
						-Scoring credits per mob <BR>
						-Partial scoring allowed (done per hour) <BR>
						-A raid can happen any time in the week - weekend or midweek. Weekend will still be the main gathering days and if you are online and there is a mob being raided you will be expected to attend. <BR>
						-Midweek raids you will not need to attend - and is counted as optional. <BR>
						-Members can run raids if approved by officers online. <BR>
						-Members running raids must adhere to the above mentioned rules on logging and sufficient raid force. <BR>
						-Members running raids may not distribute the loot. Only guild leader/officer may deal with the loot as these rules apply. <BR>
    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR><BR></td></tr>

	<tr><td><h2>2.</td><td><h2>How we share loot with allies</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>The EoD website will allow allies to login to our site and view the Raid Credits and Mob Watch (values) page. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>This enables ally members to check themselves that they have been scored and by how much for any particular raid. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>On raids an ally member can bid with his/her points as an EoD member would do given that they have sufficient funds to do so. </td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><b>Ally Loot - Summary</b><BR>
				-Allies will have a login to EoD website for mob values and raid credit screens.  <BR>
				-Allies may bid with and against EoD members for loot.  <BR>
    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR><BR></td></tr>

	<tr><td><h2>3.</td><td><h2>How does alternate characters work with EoD?</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>We do not wish to loose our current flexibility of playing alternate characters for the guilds use. Therefore we needed to find a way to reward the account holder. When you receive credits for a mob kill those credits are assigned to an 'account holder'. For example if characterA kills a mob in a raid then under the website login he may be known as characterA and you can see that he has gained points. However he may also own characterB who he also uses for guild raids. Although characterB doesn't have his own set of points and doesn't have his own website login. HOWEVER..the collection of characters belong to the same account holder. We therefore are allowing the account holder to collect points for raids that he has attended with either character.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If loot drops the character present on the raid may bid. Only that character may bid - although they can use the global account holder points (aka points he may have gainted with his alt being present on earlier raids). So characterA is on raid he can bid with the account points. If characterB is on raid he can bid the account points.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>We are NOT allowing you to bid for an alt if they are not present and active on the raid. For example if characterA is on raid and something drops and he wants characterB to loot it - you can't. You may only bid with the character that is actively on raid. Even dropable items will not be allowed to be bid for by a character on a raid and given to an alt. It should be noted that the character on the raid is the one who can bid.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>Basically the idea is that if the guild needs your cleric to play on a raid and a wizard item drops and you really want the wizard item and in addition there is say a legado wizard present on the raid you may not bid against the legado wizard. The reason is that you were asked to play the cleric and as a cleric you can bid for cleric items. You may not bid for your alt.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If in the very rare cases there is an item that has dropped and no body wants it on raid then memebrs may bid for general use - general includes (sales and alts). For example, if we raided Zlandicar and a Cowl dropped (haste head item - all/all dropable) and no one needs it as an upgrade then the guild leader/officer may announce that you can bid for it as a cash item. In this respect you use your points against anyone else to win the item do to with as you wish.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If you are repeatedly asked to play a cleric when you really want to play your wizard because you either like it or there is an item that drops from a particular mob that you never get chance to play your wizard on then you should speak with the guild leader or officer so that you are given priority on that class to play - perhaps there is another member who can play the desired class on that day. If there is no chance of defeating the mob however without you the guild would ask that you be flexible and we would take note of this and try to fill your class requirement the next time.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><b>Summary - Alt Looting</b><BR>
					-Alts may bid IF they are present, active (doing something), 58+ on a raid.<BR>
					-Alts may use the account holders points to bid for items<BR>
					-You may only bid for an item for a character if that character is present and active unless there is absolutely no character present on the raid who can use or even wants.<BR>
					-If there is no one who needs an item then the item can be put up for cash bidding. This means that anyone can bid to sell or to give to an alt.<BR>
					-To gain credits for being present on a raid that character must have an EOD tag. Characters not above 58 will not gain credits even if tagged.<BR>
					-If you have mutliple characters in the guild and the raid leader wishes you to play one for the raid you should try to be flexible and accomodate this. Bringing a level 55 Shaman instead of a level 65 cleric will not be seen as accomodating unless asked.<BR>
					-Alts may gain credits for the account holder if they are active and above level 58 on a raid.<BR>
    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR><BR></td></tr>

	<tr><td><h2>4.</td><td><h2>How does bidding work for loot?</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>Our process is basically that when something drops the guild leader/officer will handle the bidding. The process is as follows:<BR>
						Item drops<BR>
						GL/Officer calls item and asks for bids from appropriate classes that can use.  The Cost of the item is announced.<BR>
						Members tell the guild leader/officer that they wish to bid.   Points are checked.  The person with the most points that can cover the cost of the item will win.<BR>
						The GL/officer announces the winner<BR>
						The winner then loots.<BR>
						<BR>
						If no member wants to loot the item the item can be offered to members boxed characters (currently on raid playing a second char as well as a first).  If they wish to bid they can for the cost of the item.<BR>
						If no 2nd boxed characters wish to bid, then alternate characters (not currently online in the raid) may also bid for the item at the cost value.<BR>
					</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>The above process is repeated for each item. Spells are awarded to those on the level. If there is more than one member who is the level of the spell or above (and needs) they bid against each other. If a character is not sufficient level for the spell the guild bank will hold that spell until that member is that level.    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If the guild leader/officer running raid wishes to bid for an item - they can since the cost of the item is predetermined.  They obviously however must be able to cover the cost.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If the cost of an item will run the bidder into negative values - they can still take onboard the cost granted it will not send them more than -50 points into the red.</tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>If no one is willing to pay the full price or the 2nd box/alt price the item will be destroyed.  The item will not be given away for less than stated prices to a member of the raid.</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>It will be at the guild leaders descretion if an item is to be given as a gift to a non-member/non-alt of the raid if none of the members of the raid wish to bid.</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2>NOTE: You do not get to use the points until the folling day. The reason for this is purely administration. Processing points both partial and full for thirty people right after a mob has been killed while you have a corpse decay time of 7 minutes (in some cases) is crazy and unworkable.  So remember, kill a mob..you aint using those points until the next day..    </tr></td>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><b>Summary - Bidding works</b><BR>
						-We use an item/cost based bidding system.<BR>
						-Spells and other non loot items are held by bank until such level.<BR>
                        -You cannot use your new mob kill points until the following day.<BR>
						-Points on the website are final at that time. We will not do on the fly calculations for raid mobs killed that night.<BR>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>

	<tr><td><h2>5.</td><td><h2>What do you do about Key looting?</td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2><BR></td></tr>
	<tr><td colspan=2>Keys such as Sleepers, Trakanon Teeth (for VP key) etc will always be based on Raid Attendance.  This basically means the more you are online for MobKills then the higher your raid attendance will be and therefore
